{"kind":"artifact","text":"The Git commands this package runs inherit one name and are told the rest. Exact clean pushed head 937389e1c7498095073395fb094819049700ad4c on request/repo-remote-link; tree ce30bc6b, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's repair child 40862781 and my promise a5d65234.\n\nWHY A BLOCKLIST WAS ABANDONED. Four review rounds, four escapes: command-scope GIT_CONFIG_COUNT injection; GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL and GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM naming a file containing core.fsmonitor, which is a command; HOME reaching ~/.gitconfig with no GIT_CONFIG variable set at all; and then, with all three pins in place, HOME reached through a repository-local include.path whose tilde expands, XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore corrupting the status answer with no configuration file at all, and SUDO_UID widening owner-trust under root. I verified the include.path vector myself. Each closed vector revealed the next, because a blocklist is a claim that I have enumerated how Git reaches executable configuration, and I was wrong about that four times running.\n\nTHE CONTRACT. gitInheritedEnvironment admits exactly one name, PATH. Everything else is absent by construction rather than by enumeration. gitStatedEnvironment then says HOME=os.DevNull, XDG_CONFIG_HOME=os.DevNull, GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=os.DevNull, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=os.DevNull. os.DevNull rather than an empty directory because a path that cannot hold a directory entry cannot be raced or filled between two commands of one projection, and needs no creation, cleanup or error path in a constructor with nowhere to report one. SUDO_UID is neutralised by absence, which is what an allowlist gives for free — Git's own documentation prescribes exactly this. Name comparison folds case, because Windows spells the admitted name Path and an exact compare would drop the real PATH there; and internal/app does build for Windows, which I checked.\n\nTHE PATH ADMISSION IS THE WEAKEST PART AND I WANT IT JUDGED. All five commands complete with no environment whatever — I measured this myself with env -i on darwin/git 2.50.1, and the implementer measured the same on linux/git 2.53.0. So nothing here was observed to need PATH. It is admitted on prospective grounds: Git resolves subprograms through PATH by design, and a command added later would otherwise fail looking like a broken repository. By the rule this row was built on — if you cannot say why a command needs it, it does not go in — PATH does not survive. I did not remove it because planner's guidance named it and because I twice tonight reasoned one step ahead about this surface and was wrong; the measurement is in the artifact so a reviewer can decide rather than take my word.\n\nWHAT REMAINS ADMITTED BY NECESSITY, AND IS AN EXECUTION CHANNEL. The repository's own configuration file. It has to be read — remote.origin.url lives there — and core.fsmonitor set in it executes. I checked whether removing PATH would shrink that: it does not. Git runs fsmonitor through a shell that has a compiled-in default path, so the command executes with no PATH inherited at all. That is a real remaining exposure for anyone who points this at a repository they did not create, and I would rather state it than let the allowlist imply the boundary is closed.\n\nWHAT IS GIVEN UP. These five reads now refuse under sudo even for a repository owned by the invoking user, because the ownership allowances Git would otherwise take from the environment do not reach them. Read-only projection failures are visible errors and the citation guard fails closed, but this is an operational change beyond what the request text described.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, each restored and re-verified. Admitting HOME while no longer stating it fails the include.path subtest; the same for XDG_CONFIG_HOME and the two ignore subtests; admitting both fails all three. Admitting GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL fails its own subtest. Changing the fold comparison to == fails the case-varied test only. Admitting SUDO_UID or GIT_DIR fails their named tests. The citation exit-code distinction still fails its own tests in both directions, and it still breaks the lookup through the repository's own configuration file, which the allowlist leaves admitted by necessity.\n\nTHE CONTROLS ARE THE PART THAT MAKES THE REST TRUSTWORTHY. Every vector regression carries a control run outside the constructor that fails hard, never skips, if the exploit stops reproducing — so a Git that closes a vector surfaces as a test needing a rewrite rather than as continued false reassurance. Two of the tests assert on the constructed environment rather than on a status answer: the case-varied one, because on darwin and linux Git's getenv is case-sensitive so there is no exploit to demonstrate, and the SUDO_UID one, whose ownership consequence is not exercised at all because it needs root and a second uid. Both say so in their comments rather than implying coverage.\n\nNOTED AND NOT BUILT. This mechanism should later be shared with internal/gitstore rather than duplicated. That belongs to planner's separate request d2605fd7, gated on a threat-model ruling from hugh, and building it here would decide that question by implementing it.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 227.843s, internal/app 161.580s, internal/kernel 157.884s, internal/docset 101.461s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 94.811s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ exit 0, since the case rule only bites where the OS folds environment names; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux — this suite ran on darwin/arm64 with git 2.50.1.","body":{"commit":"937389e1c7498095073395fb094819049700ad4c","path":"internal/app"}}